## What changed

**R2 — streak state, incident collapse, notification policy.** A failure now joins a streak keyed on its own message (`failure_streak`, `streak_message`, `streak_started_at`, `last_notified_streak`, plus `last_notified_at` because the count alone can't say whether the hourly allowance is spent) added to `timer_runtime` through the existing `PRAGMA table_info` + `ALTER TABLE` pattern. Repeats fold onto the row already filed via `bump_incident`, which increments `count` and `last_seen_at`; `add_incident` keeps its insert-only mute behaviour untouched, and `record_incident` is the bump-then-add wrapper the service calls. The incident message dropped `scheduled_at` — it is the key repeats fold onto, and a per-run key files a per-run row. Alerts fire at streak 1, 3, 10 then hourly; from streak 2 the "started" and status Slack posts stop; a success ending a streak of 3+ sends one recovery message. `notifier.log_sent` writes `notify.sent timer=<id> kind=<k> streak=<n>` per delivery, the daily Slack thread id is persisted in `meta` keyed by day, and `_report_stalled_waits` files one `info` incident per timer stuck on exit 75 for a day (hooked into the hourly housekeeping next to `_prune`).

One deviation worth knowing: the gated alert reaches Slack only from streak 2 up. At streak 1 the run's own `:x: Failed` reply already carries it, and posting both added a *fourth* Slack message to a single failed run — `UntilConditionTests::test_until_no_slack_when_timer_survives` caught that, and it is the opposite of the point.

**R4 — the flap breaker.** `DAEMON_HEALTHY_UPTIME_SECONDS` 60 → 300. No new daemon status, so the `ds.status == "stopped"` gate at `service.py:2597` is untouched.

⚠️ `DaemonTimerTests::test_shutdown_terminated_daemon_is_not_reported_as_failure` is flaky on this machine and **not** from my change: 3 failures in 8 runs on my branch, 2 in 8 on stashed clean code, same 60s signature every time. `stop()` only terminates runs in `_active_processes`, so when it lands in the pre-Popen window the run row already says `started`, the `sleep 60` is never killed, `_executor.shutdown(wait=True)` blocks for the full minute and the run ends `success`. That is the same pre-Popen window `9754fbd` addressed for the ghost check, and fixing `stop()` belongs to the spawn-race brief, not to R2/R4 — so I left it and am reporting it.

